# Two decisions in eight bytes, and both are places a plausible walk goes
# wrong in the flattering direction - it still parses, just not into this.
#
# `\n` is an escape php's grammar owns, so the content run ENDS in front of
# the backslash and `escape_sequence` is a sibling of `string_content`, not a
# substring of it. A walk that treated a backslash as "skip two bytes and keep
# going" would produce one 5-byte `string_content` here and a tree that still
# has an `encapsed_string` in it - which is why `holds escape_sequence` and
# the two spans are claimed and not just the outer node.
#
# `$1` is NOT a variable. php's `is_valid_name_char` admits digits, but the
# scanner's `$` arm requires the byte after the dollar to be a name char that
# is not a digit - so `$1x` is three bytes of ordinary content. This is the
# rule that makes every `preg_replace` replacement string in the corpus text
# rather than an interpolation, and getting it backwards costs a run per
# occurrence.
#
# Claims are tree-sitter's tree over these bytes.
roots 1
mends 0
holds encapsed_string
holds escape_sequence
lacks text
lacks variable_name_in_string
spans escape_sequence 12 14
spans string_content 14 17
